.TH PAM_HOWDY 8 "@HOWDY_MAN_DATE@" "Howdy Next @PROJECT_VERSION@" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
pam_howdy \- PAM facial authentication module for Howdy Next
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pam_howdy.so
[\fBworkaround=\fR\fIvalue\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pam_howdy.so
authenticates a user with Howdy Next facial recognition.
It reads
.I @HOWDY_CONFIG_PATH_ROFF@
and enrolled user models from
.IR @HOWDY_USER_MODELS_DIR_ROFF@ ,
which must stay root-owned and non-user-writable.
.PP
When the caller passes
.BR PAM_SILENT ,
status, error, and confirmation messages are suppressed; password prompts are
unaffected.
.SH OPTIONS
.B workaround=
is configured per service on the
.B pam_howdy.so
line (not in
.IR config.ini ).
Only the first
.B workaround=
argument is used.
Empty or unknown values, including
.BR workaround=off ,
keep it off.
@PAM_WORKAROUND_REFERENCE@
.SH "WORKAROUND MODES"
Workaround modes run face recognition alongside the password prompt and cancel
it on a match.
They do not start if a PAM authentication token already exists.
If the original PAM conversation cannot be restored afterward, Howdy installs a
fail-closed fallback (quarantining unsafe callbacks) and returns
.BR PAM_SYSTEM_ERR .
.TP
.B workaround=native
For interactive terminals such as
.BR sudo (8)
and
.BR login (1).
Requires
.B PAM_TTY
to be the foreground terminal and to match at least one of stdin, stdout, or
stderr; otherwise it disables itself and uses the normal PAM prompt (never
falling back to
.BR input ).
Reads the password in canonical mode with the terminal's
.BR stty (1)
editing keys (literal-next disabled;
.BR ^C ,
.BR ^Z ,
or empty
.B ^D
cancel at once;
.B ^\e
cancels on Enter; piped stdin such as
.B sudo \-S
is ignored).
If the process is killed mid-prompt, run
.B stty sane
to restore the terminal.
.TP
.B workaround=input
For graphical prompts without a TTY.
Watches for a hidden-input prompt and injects at most one Enter via
.I /dev/uinput
after a face match.
Because
.I /dev/uinput
sends to whichever window has focus, validate each service before enabling; if
it misses, finish typing the password manually.
Falls back to the normal PAM prompt if
.I /dev/uinput
is unavailable.
.TP
.B workaround=native\-input
Tries
.B native
first and falls back to
.B input
when no usable TTY is available.
.SH SETUP
.SS Basic
.nf
auth  sufficient  pam_howdy.so
.fi
.SS "TTY Services (sudo, login)"
Place Howdy before
.BR pam_unix (8)
so face recognition runs alongside the password prompt:
.PP
.nf
auth  sufficient  pam_howdy.so workaround=native
auth  sufficient  pam_unix.so try_first_pass nullok
.fi
.SS "Graphical Prompts (Polkit)"
.nf
auth  sufficient  pam_howdy.so workaround=native\-input
auth  sufficient  pam_unix.so try_first_pass nullok
.fi
.SS "Pre-collected Passwords (e.g. waylock)"
For lock screens that read the password before calling PAM, check the password
first and omit
.BR workaround= :
.PP
.nf
auth  sufficient  pam_unix.so try_first_pass nullok
auth  sufficient  pam_howdy.so
.fi
.SH "SECURITY NOTES"
Keep
.I @HOWDY_CONFIG_DIR_ROFF@
and
.I @HOWDY_CONFIG_PATH_ROFF@
root-controlled; never make them world-readable.
Unprivileged PAM callers (such as lock screens) use setuid
.B howdy\-auth\-helper
to open read-only file descriptors and pass them back over a private Unix
socket.
.PP
.B abort_if_ssh
is a convenience, not a security boundary.
It checks only caller-controlled environment variables
.RB ( SSH_CONNECTION ,
.BR SSH_CLIENT ,
.BR SSH_TTY ,
.BR SSHD_OPTS ),
which a remote user can unset before running
.BR sudo (8).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I @HOWDY_CONFIG_PATH_ROFF@
Configuration file.
.TP
.I @HOWDY_USER_MODELS_DIR_ROFF@
Enrolled user face models.
.TP
.I @HOWDY_AUTH_HELPER_PATH_ROFF@
Setuid helper for unprivileged PAM callers.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR howdy (1),
.BR howdy.ini (5),
.BR pam (8),
.BR pam_unix (8)
